WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.930
Your data model as a diagram, saved as DBML files in your repo.

2
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.546
Edit it by hand, or with your AI assistant,

3
00:00:07.596 --> 00:00:10.562
through a built-in skill it uses by itself.

4
00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:12.389
Already have a model?

5
00:00:12.439 --> 00:00:16.057
Bring it in from the tools most teams use: Databricks,

6
00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:20.193
Snowflake, Power BI and the most popular SQL databases.

7
00:00:21.212 --> 00:00:25.413
Getting the DDL out is one step, for a whole schema or database.

8
00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:28.845
From PostgreSQL, pg_dump writes it to a file.

9
00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:34.239
From Snowflake, it's one query, GET_DDL, saved as a .sql file.

10
00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:38.477
Then click New, Import SQL DDL, and import the folder.

11
00:00:38.527 --> 00:00:42.929
It works out the dialect: PostgreSQL, one of the nine it reads.

12
00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:46.189
You get a draft of every table and relationship.

13
00:00:46.739 --> 00:00:48.691
Keep it, and it's your diagram.

14
00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:54.917
Each table is now a DBML file, an open format, next to the code that builds it.

15
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.920
Change it, and Git shows exactly what changed.

16
00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:04.552
Big models stay readable: this one has 2,000 tables and 2,700 relationships.

17
00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:08.824
Every line finds its way around the tables in the background, in seconds.

18
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:13.102
Zoom in, and hover a table: arrows flow along its relationships.

19
00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.284
Edit it by hand, on the canvas.

20
00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:21.013
Add a table, with starter columns if you like, and lay it out.

21
00:01:21.063 --> 00:01:23.131
Every edit is saved to its file.

22
00:01:23.581 --> 00:01:27.212
Or ask your AI assistant. Click Set Up My AI Helper.

23
00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:32.777
It adds a skill: plain markdown files that teach your assistant ERD Studio's format.

24
00:01:33.227 --> 00:01:36.219
Ask Claude Code for a change, in plain words.

25
00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:38.805
It loads the ERD Studio skill by itself,

26
00:01:38.855 --> 00:01:42.423
and a pre-tool hook makes sure it has, before any edit,

27
00:01:42.473 --> 00:01:45.771
so what it writes is a diagram you can see, right away.

28
00:01:48.727 --> 00:01:53.449
Here's the change: a new table file, and the line that links it to the orders table.

29
00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:56.460
On the canvas, the new table and its relationship.

30
00:01:58.327 --> 00:02:02.491
The design change goes in the same pull request as the code that builds it,

31
00:02:02.541 --> 00:02:04.689
so your team reviews both together.

32
00:02:06.927 --> 00:02:12.578
Your repo is the single source of truth: just DBML files, no modelling database.

33
00:02:12.628 --> 00:02:15.832
ERD Studio for Confluence reads them from GitHub,

34
00:02:15.882 --> 00:02:18.888
and shows the diagram to business users.

35
00:02:20.227 --> 00:02:23.386
ERD Studio is free to use in VS Code.
